"Surah al-Inshirah begins by recalling the great blessings bestowed upon the Prophet. In the first ayah, it is stated, 'Did We not expand your breast for you?' Here, the expression 'sharh al-sadr' (expansion of the breast) signifies bringing relief to the heart, strength to the soul, and joy, rather than a physical opening of the chest. As Raghib also stated, what is in question here is a divine light and tranquility of heart.\n\nLexicographers elaborate on this expression. According to the author of Shihab, while 'sharh' originally meant cutting open flesh, over time it came to be used in the sense of removing the heart's distress and bringing joy. Alusi, on the other hand, states that the origin of sharh is dispersing and expanding, and that it may be regarded as conventionally literal in the sense of the soul's relief. These explanations help us understand the spiritual expansion in the ayah.\n\nExegetes mention two main views regarding 'sharh al-sadr': The first is the physical operation mentioned in the narrations, in which the chest was opened and the heart removed and filled with faith and wisdom during childhood, at the onset of prophethood, or on the night of the Mi'raj. The second is that this was a spiritual opening, which is the meaning agreed upon by all. The essential purpose is the unfolding of the truth through faith and wisdom. The event in the hadith of the Mi'raj is also an example of this spiritual opening.\n\nIn the second ayah, it is stated, 'And did We not remove from you your burden?' 'Wizr' means a heavy burden; although some have interpreted it as sin, the majority understand it as the difficulties, perplexity, and anxiety of responsibility prior to or at the beginning of prophethood. This burden was so heavy that it weighed down the Messenger of Allah's back and made his bones creak. Allah Almighty lifted this burden with the revelation of Surah ad-Duha and brought relief to his heart.\n\nIn the fourth ayah, it is stated, 'And did We not raise high your mention?' Here, 'dhikr' signifies fame and honor. The name of the Prophet is mentioned alongside the name of Allah; in the adhan, in prayer, and wherever Allah is mentioned, he is mentioned as well. In a hadith, Gabriel reported that Allah said, 'Whenever I am mentioned, you too will be mentioned with Me.' This is the highest station of honor.\n\nIn the fifth and sixth ayahs, it is stated, 'For indeed, with hardship comes ease.' This statement is emphasized by being repeated twice. 'Hardship' ('usr) appears as definite, while 'ease' (yusr) appears as indefinite. This indicates that following or alongside every hardship there is ease, and indeed two eases. According to a narration, when this ayah was revealed, the Prophet said, 'One hardship cannot overcome two eases.'\n\nIn the seventh ayah, it is stated, 'So when you are free, still toil.' That is, when you finish one task, immediately occupy yourself with another act of worship, and do not turn to comfort. When the obligatory acts are finished, move on to the supererogatory; when the prayer is finished, stand in supplication. In the eighth ayah, there is the command, 'And to your Lord alone turn your attention.' In all your strivings, aim only for Allah; do not turn to anyone other than Him. See the Bestower of blessings in the blessing and walk toward Him.\n\nThe maxims derived from these surahs are as follows: 'When there is constriction, expansion follows', 'Blessing corresponds to burden', 'The judgment of an action is according to its intended purpose', and 'Whoever strives for the sake of Allah reaches Allah.' These two surahs (ad-Duha and al-Inshirah), by describing the condition and glory of the Prophet, recall the diffusion of spiritual grace, and then, by turning from the blessing to the Bestower of blessings, remind us of yearning for Allah alone. Following them comes Surah at-Tin."